WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.000
Moving from the web to mobile changes the environment, but the underlying logic you use to verify your code follows a familiar path.

2
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:13.000
Your existing experience with GEST and the testing library philosophy carries over directly.

3
00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:18.000
The core goal, testing user behavior rather than internal code, is identical.

4
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:21.000
The differences are found in the specific syntax.

5
00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:29.000
Because mobile apps do not use the web's document object model, standard web-based automated tests will fail until we adjust for the native environment.

6
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:37.000
We are going to build a test suite for a react native login form, first the initial render, next validation errors, and finally a successful submission.

7
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:42.000
By adjusting these syntax patterns, you can apply web testing skills to native components with precision.

8
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:49.000
This mockup shows our testing target, a basic mobile screen featuring an email input, a password input, and a submit button.

9
00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:51.000
Just remains our test runner.

10
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:57.000
With testing library, we focus on user interaction with these fields, rather than internal state management.

11
00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:00.000
The first shift is how we target these elements.

12
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:05.000
In this environment, we cannot use standard HTML tags like div or input.

13
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:09.000
On the web, we isolate elements with data-testID.

14
00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:13.000
In React Native, this is replaced by the testID property on a text input.

15
00:01:13.000 --> 00:01:20.000
We render the form and use these identifiers to assert the fields are present, confirming it loads without errors.

16
00:01:20.000 --> 00:01:23.000
Running this in the terminal confirms a passing initial state.

17
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:29.000
Identifying elements with these native properties is the first step adapting web habits for mobile.

18
00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:34.000
Next, we need to test the form's validation by simulating invalid user inputs.

19
00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:40.000
This requires a major change in how we simulate typing, as the standard web chain event will not work here.

20
00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:43.000
Web typing triggers a generic DOM chain event.

21
00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:47.000
In React Native, we must use events compatible with native touch inputs.

22
00:01:47.000 --> 00:01:52.000
To interact with a text input, we use the change text method.

23
00:01:52.000 --> 00:01:56.000
This specific event processes string updates in React Native.

24
00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:03.000
We use it to leave a field blank, simulate pressing submit, and assert that the required message is visible.

25
00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:07.000
The test runner confirms that the error logic is working as intended.

26
00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:15.000
Using these specific event bindings allows the test suite to accurately reflect how a user physically interacts with the touch screen.

27
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:20.000
To complete the suite, we verify the path where the user provides valid information.

28
00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:25.000
We use change text again to inject a valid email and password into the fields.

29
00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:29.000
We simulate the final tap, verifying data acceptance and submission.

30
00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:36.000
Running the full suite shows all three scenarios, initial, error, and success passing in order.

31
00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:41.000
Verifying these states ensures the component handles common user interactions before it is deployed.

32
00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:45.000
This reference card summarizes the shifts we've made.

33
00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:53.000
We swap standard inputs for text input, use change text for events, and identify elements with test ID.

34
00:02:53.000 --> 00:03:00.000
These adjustments allow you to test React Native components with the same rigor you apply to web components.

35
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:10.000
As your application grows, you may need to move beyond component logic and test the entire app on a physical device or emulator using a tool called detox.

36
00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:18.000
While our current tests verify isolated logic, detox provides automated end-to-end testing for the fully compiled application.

37
00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:28.000
The specific tools and syntax change between operating systems, but the practice of testing against user behavior remains a consistent standard for production-ready code.

